{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "target": "ES2022",
    "lib": ["ES2023"],
    "module": "ESNext",
    "skipLibCheck": true,
    "moduleResolution": "bundler",
    "allowImportingTsExtensions": true,
    "isolatedModules": true,
    "moduleDetection": "force",
    "noEmit": true,
    "strict": true,
    "baseUrl": ".",
    "paths": { "@/*": ["./src/*"] }
  },
  "include": ["vite.config.ts", "scripts"],
  /*
    THE SEED PROOF IS RUN, NOT TYPE CHECKED WITH THE BUILD TOOLING.

    scripts/proof-cup.ts imports application source so it can validate the
    bracket against the real seed, which is exactly what makes it worth
    keeping and exactly what makes it dangerous here. This project is for
    vite.config.ts and the build scripts; it has no jsx setting, because
    nothing in it should ever reach a component. Pulling app source in
    means one later import of a provider turns `tsc -b` red for a reason
    that has nothing to do with the application, which has already
    happened once and cost a debugging session.

    So it is excluded from the project and run on its own with tsx, as its
    own header says. The proof keeps its teeth and the build keeps its
    footing.
  */
  "exclude": ["scripts/proof-cup.ts", "scripts/proof-accounts.ts"]
}
